<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">League of Nations mandate</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as mandates</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> mandate was a territory taken from the defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and placed under the administration of an Allied power. Mandatory powers were supposed to govern in the interests of the inhabitants under League supervision. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> held several mandates, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, as well as former German colonies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/league-of-nations-mandate.jpg" alt="League of Nations mandate" width="200" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:League_of_Nations_mandate_Middle_East_and_Africa.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Australia (1901), New Zealand and South Africa (1910) — which had elected parliaments and substantial autonomy — to directly administered Crown Colonies, the Indian Raj governed by a viceroy and the Indian Civil Service, and the protectorates and <b>League of Nations mandates</b> administered under varying degrees of oversight.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Its direct colonial legacy was modest, comprising German East Africa, German South West Africa, Togo, Cameroon, the leased territory of Kiautschou in China, and Pacific island territories, all stripped by the Treaty of Versailles and largely redistributed as <b>League of Nations mandates</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After the First World War Belgium gained Eupen and Malmedy from Germany and the <b>League of Nations mandate</b> of Ruanda-Urundi, where its administration hardened the distinction between Hutu and Tutsi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Kingdom of Belgium · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Belgium occupied the country in 1916 and from 1922 governed it as part of Ruanda-Urundi, a <b>League of Nations mandate</b> and later a United Nations trust territory, keeping the monarchy but making the mwami a figurehead and favouring ganwa and Tutsi as chiefs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-burundi">Kingdom of Burundi · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1916 Belgian troops from the Congo occupied the country, and from 1922 Belgium governed it as part of Ruanda-Urundi, a <b>League of Nations mandate</b> and later United Nations trust territory.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-rwanda">Kingdom of Rwanda · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Mandatory Palestine was Britain's <b>League of Nations mandate</b> over Palestine from 1920 to 1948, charged with a Jewish national home and self-government for a population then 88 per cent Arab; Jewish immigration, the Arab revolt of 1936–1939 and the Jewish insurgency of 1944–1948 ended in the UN partition…</blockquote><a href="/empire/mandatory-palestine">Mandatory Palestine · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
